public static String canTravelMaze(int n, int[] start, int[] end, int[][] paths) {
    boolean[][] visited = new boolean[n][n];
    Queue<int[]> queue = new LinkedList<>();
    queue.offer(start);
    visited[start[0]][start[1]] = true;
    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
        int[] current = queue.poll();
        if (current[0] == end[0] && current[1] == end[1]) {
            return "can be travelled";
        }
        for (int[] path : paths) {
            int nextRow = current[0] + path[0];
            int nextCol = current[1] + path[1];
            if (nextRow >= 0 && nextRow < n && nextCol >= 0 && nextCol < n && !visited[nextRow][nextCol]) {
                visited[nextRow][nextCol] = true;
                queue.offer(new int[]{nextRow, nextCol});
            }
        }
    }
    return "cannot be travelled";
}
```
This function uses a breadth-first search (BFS) algorithm to check if the maze can be travelled from the starting n-tersection to the ending n-tersection. It maintains a 2D boolean array `visited` to keep track of the n-tersections that have been visited, a queue `queue` to keep track of the n-tersections that need to be visited next, and a 2D array `paths` to store the paths between adjacent n-tersections.

The function first adds the starting n-tersection to the queue and marks it as visited. Then, it iterates through the queue while there are n-tersections left to visit. For each n-tersection, it checks if it is the ending n-tersection, and if so, it returns "can be travelled". If not, it iterates through the paths for the current n-tersection and checks if the next n-tersection is valid and not visited. If it is, it marks it as visited and adds it to the queue. If all the paths for the current n-tersection have been checked, it removes it from the queue.

If the queue is empty and the ending n-tersection has not been reached, the function returns "cannot be travelled".

Note that this function assumes that the maze is a 2D grid and that the n-tersection coordinates are represented as a 2-element array with the row index and column index respectively. It also assumes that the paths between adjacent n-tersections are represented as a 2D array with each element being a 2-element array representing the row and column difference between the n-tersections.

